window.HDH_SEARCH=[{"t":"Getting started","s":"","u":"docs/getting-started/","x":"Expenity keeps your bills and subscriptions in one place and tells you what is coming up. This guide takes about two minutes."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Open Expenity","u":"docs/getting-started/#open-expenity","x":"Open the app at george-stathopoulos.github.io/expenity/app/. There is nothing to install and no account to create. It works in any modern browser on a phone, tablet or computer. To keep it on your home screen like an app, see Install on your device. !The first screen, with templates to start from."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Add your first bill","u":"docs/getting-started/#add-your-first-bill","x":"Press Add bill . You have three ways to start: - Type a sentence , for example Netflix 15.99 monthly on the 3rd . The form below fills itself and the chips show what Expenity understood. More examples. - Pick a template such as Rent, Electricity or Car insurance. It sets the name, category and how often it repeats. You add the amount. - Fill in the form yourself. Check the form and press Save bill . The bill appears on the Today screen. !Adding a bill by typing a sentence. If you want to look around first, press Load sample bills on the empty screen. You can delete them later with Settings → D"},{"t":"Getting started","s":"The Today screen","u":"docs/getting-started/#the-today-screen","x":"Today shows three totals (overdue, the next 7 days, the rest of the month) and your bills grouped by when they are due. Overdue bills are red and come first. !The Today screen. On each bill: - Paid records the payment and moves a repeating bill to its next date. Mark paid. - ⋯ opens snooze, skip, payment link, copy reference, edit and delete. - Tapping the bill opens it for editing."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Get reminded","u":"docs/getting-started/#get-reminded","x":"Expenity reminds you in two ways, and it is worth setting up the second one: see Reminders."},{"t":"Getting started","s":"Where to go next","u":"docs/getting-started/#where-to-go-next","x":"- Add bills: sentences, templates, repeats and variable amounts - Reminders: notifications and the calendar file - Calendar and insights: what is due, and what is coming - Privacy and your data: where your bills live and how to back them up"},{"t":"Add bills","s":"","u":"docs/add-bills/","x":"Every bill has a name, an optional amount, a next due date and how often it repeats. Everything else is optional."},{"t":"Add bills","s":"Type a sentence","u":"docs/add-bills/#type-a-sentence","x":"At the top of the Add dialog, type what you pay in your own words. Expenity picks out the name, the amount, the currency, how often it repeats and the date, in English or in Greek. The chips under the box show what it understood, and the form fills as you type. Press Enter to jump to the form and check it. You type You get Netflix 15.99 monthly on the 3rd Netflix, 15.99, every month, next 3rd Rent €850 every month on 1st Rent, 850 EUR, every month, next 1st Electricity ~60 monthly 15th Electricity, about 60, every month, next 15th Car insurance 420 yearly 12 march Car insurance, 420, every yea"},{"t":"Add bills","s":"Use a template","u":"docs/add-bills/#use-a-template","x":"Under the sentence box there are templates: Rent, Electricity, Water, Internet, Mobile phone, Netflix, Spotify, Car insurance, Credit card, Gym, Property tax and School fees (in Greek: Ενοίκιο, ΔΕΗ, ΕΥΔΑΠ, ΕΝΦΙΑ and so on). A template fills in the name, the category and the repeat. Then add the amount and the date."},{"t":"Add bills","s":"The form","u":"docs/add-bills/#the-form","x":"Field What it does Name, Amount, Currency Amount is optional. Currency is a three-letter code, such as EUR. Next due date The date of the next payment. Repeats Once, every week, every 2 weeks, every month, every 3 months, every year. A bill due on the 31st stays on the last day of shorter months and returns to the 31st. Category Used for the icons and the spending chart. Remind me From the day itself up to 2 weeks before. \"Default\" follows the setting in Settings. The amount changes each time Marks the amount as an estimate (shown with ~). Paid automatically For direct debits. The button says "},{"t":"Add bills","s":"Mark a bill paid","u":"docs/add-bills/#mark-a-bill-paid","x":"Press Paid . Check the amount and the date and confirm. For a repeating bill the next due date is worked out for you. A one-off bill is finished. A message offers Undo for a few seconds. !Marking a bill as paid. The payments you record feed the insights: spending by category and price changes."},{"t":"Add bills","s":"Snooze, skip, edit, delete","u":"docs/add-bills/#snooze-skip-edit-delete","x":"In the ⋯ menu: - Snooze pauses the notifications for that bill for a day or three. The due date does not change. - Skip this time moves a repeating bill to its next date without recording a payment. - Edit and Delete do what they say. Deleting can be undone for a few seconds."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"","u":"docs/reminders/","x":"A website cannot wake your phone when it is closed. That is a limit of how browsers work, and Expenity does not hide it. So there are two kinds of reminder, and they work together."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"1. Your calendar (works when Expenity is closed)","u":"docs/reminders/#1-your-calendar-works-when-expenity-is-closed","x":"This is the reminder to rely on. 1. Open Settings . 2. Under Reminders, press Download calendar file . 3. Import the file into your calendar app. The file holds every open bill as an all-day event, with its repeat rule and two alarms: at 09:00 on the reminder day you chose, and at 09:00 on the due day. Your calendar app then does the reminding on every device where that calendar syncs. How to import: - Google Calendar (on a computer): Settings → Import & export → Import, choose the file. - Apple Calendar : open the file, or File → Import, and choose a calendar. - Outlook : File → Open & Export"},{"t":"Reminders","s":"2. Notifications while Expenity is open","u":"docs/reminders/#2-notifications-while-expenity-is-open","x":"In Settings → Notifications while Expenity is open , press Turn on and allow notifications when your browser asks. When you open Expenity or keep it in a tab, you get one notification a day for bills that are due within their reminder window and are not snoozed. This does not work when the app is fully closed. For that, use the calendar file."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Choose how early","u":"docs/reminders/#choose-how-early","x":"In Settings choose the Default reminder , from the day itself up to two weeks before. Each bill can override it in the Remind me field."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"Snooze","u":"docs/reminders/#snooze","x":"In a bill's ⋯ menu, Snooze to tomorrow or Snooze 3 days stops its notifications for a while."},{"t":"Reminders","s":"What is coming","u":"docs/reminders/#what-is-coming","x":"Push notifications and email reminders when the app is closed need a small server, together with syncing between devices. They are planned; see the roadmap."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/","x":""},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"The calendar","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#the-calendar","x":"The Calendar tab shows one month at a time. Each day with a bill due has coloured dots (amber for due, red if it is overdue, green for a payment you made) and, on wider screens, the total due that day. Press a day to see its bills below, and use the arrows to change month. The total for the month is at the top right. !The month calendar. Repeating bills appear on every date they fall on, not only the next one, so you can look months ahead."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Insights","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#insights","x":"The Insights tab answers \"what is coming, and where does it go?\". !The insights page."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Next 30, 60 and 90 days","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#next-30-60-and-90-days","x":"Three totals of what is due, with the number of payments. They are built from your open bills and their repeats."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Coming out, week by week","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#coming-out-week-by-week","x":"Thirteen columns, one per week, starting today. The height is the amount due that week. Hover over a column for the exact number. If you use more than one currency the columns add them together as plain numbers, so use it as a shape rather than an exact figure."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Price changes","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#price-changes","x":"When the last two payments you recorded for a bill differ by 5% or more, Expenity says so, for example \"Electricity went from €58 to €64 (+10%)\". This only works if you record the real amount when you mark a bill paid."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Paid this year, by category","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#paid-this-year-by-category","x":"How much you paid this year in each category, as bars with percentages. It counts the payments you marked paid in Expenity, so it grows as you use the app."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Subscriptions","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#subscriptions","x":"Bills in the Subscriptions category that repeat are shown with what they cost over a year, and the total. Fifteen euros a month is 180 a year."},{"t":"Calendar and insights","s":"Currencies","u":"docs/calendar-and-insights/#currencies","x":"Each bill has its own currency. Totals are shown per currency (for example \"€120 + $15\"). Expenity does not convert between currencies."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/","x":"Expenity is a web app. You can use it in a browser tab, or install it so it opens in its own window with its own icon, and works offline."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"iPhone and iPad","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#iphone-and-ipad","x":"1. Open Expenity in Safari . 2. Tap the Share button. 3. Choose Add to Home Screen ."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Android","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#android","x":"1. Open Expenity in Chrome . 2. Open the menu (⋮). 3. Choose Install app (or Add to Home screen )."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Computer","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#computer","x":"In Chrome or Edge, click the install icon at the right end of the address bar, or open the menu and choose Install Expenity . Safari on a Mac: File → Add to Dock."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"Offline","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#offline","x":"After the first visit the app files are stored on your device, so Expenity opens and works without a connection. Your bills are stored on the device too. If a new version is available it updates the next time you open it with a connection."},{"t":"Install on your device","s":"One device at a time","u":"docs/install-on-your-device/#one-device-at-a-time","x":"Your data lives in the browser on the device you use. A phone and a laptop do not share bills yet. Until sync exists, pick one main device, and use the calendar file for reminders on others: see Reminders. You can move your bills between devices with a backup: see Privacy and your data."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/","x":""},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Your data stays on your device","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#your-data-stays-on-your-device","x":"Your bills and payments are saved in your browser's local storage on the device you are using. They are not sent to a server, because Expenity has no server that stores them. There is no account, no sign-in and no profile. The website and the app count visits anonymously with Cloudflare Web Analytics: no cookies and no personal data, and never the contents of your bills. Expenity never asks for bank details, never connects to a bank and never moves money. The payment link on a bill just opens the page you saved."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Back up and restore","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#back-up-and-restore","x":"Because the data is in your browser, clearing the site's data (or losing the device) removes it. Keep a backup. In Settings → Your data : - Download backup (JSON) saves everything: bills, payments and settings. - Restore a backup reads such a file and replaces the current data (after you confirm). This is also how you move your bills to another device. - Payments as CSV exports your payment history for a spreadsheet. !The settings page."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Delete everything","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#delete-everything","x":"Settings → Delete all my data removes all bills and payments from this browser. It cannot be undone, so download a backup first if you might want them back."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Notifications","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#notifications","x":"If you turn notifications on, your browser asks permission and shows them locally. Nothing is sent anywhere."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"The calendar file","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#the-calendar-file","x":"The calendar file you download contains your bill names, amounts and notes. When you import it into Google, Apple or Outlook calendar, it is stored by that service under its own privacy rules. Do not put sensitive details in notes if that matters to you."},{"t":"Privacy and your data","s":"Open source","u":"docs/privacy-and-your-data/#open-source","x":"The code is public, so you can check all of this: github.com/george-stathopoulos/expenity."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"","u":"docs/faq/","x":""},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Is Expenity free?","u":"docs/faq/#is-expenity-free","x":"Yes, and it is open source. There are no accounts, ads or paid tiers today."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it work on my iPhone, Android phone and computer?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-work-on-my-iphone-android-phone-and-computer","x":"Yes. It is a web app that runs in any modern browser, and you can install it: see Install on your device. Your data is stored per device, though."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Can I see my bills on my phone and my laptop?","u":"docs/faq/#can-i-see-my-bills-on-my-phone-and-my-laptop","x":"Not automatically yet. Syncing needs a server and is planned. For now, download a backup on one device and restore it on the other, and use the calendar file for reminders everywhere."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Will it remind me when the app is closed?","u":"docs/faq/#will-it-remind-me-when-the-app-is-closed","x":"Through your calendar, yes: download the calendar file from Settings and import it. Expenity itself can only notify you while it is open, because a website cannot wake a phone on its own. Push and email reminders are planned. See Reminders."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Why not just connect to my bank?","u":"docs/faq/#why-not-just-connect-to-my-bank","x":"Reading a bank account would make Expenity handle credentials and sensitive data, which we would rather avoid. You tell it what you pay, it tells you when."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Where are my bills stored? What if I clear my browser?","u":"docs/faq/#where-are-my-bills-stored-what-if-i-clear-my-browser","x":"In your browser on this device. Clearing site data removes them, so use Settings → Download backup regularly."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Can several people share the bills?","u":"docs/faq/#can-several-people-share-the-bills","x":"Not yet. Shared households are planned."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Does it support Greek?","u":"docs/faq/#does-it-support-greek","x":"Yes. Press ΕΛ at the top, or choose the language in Settings. The app, this help center and the website are in English and Greek, and the sentence box understands both."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Which currencies?","u":"docs/faq/#which-currencies","x":"Any currency by its three-letter code. Totals are shown per currency; there is no conversion."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"The sentence box got something wrong.","u":"docs/faq/#the-sentence-box-got-something-wrong","x":"It is a helper. Correct the field in the form below it. If you have a sentence that should work, please open an issue with the exact text."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"How do I report a bug or suggest something?","u":"docs/faq/#how-do-i-report-a-bug-or-suggest-something","x":"On GitHub."}];